# Runs the two CHEAP checks CI runs, against the WHOLE tree, before anything
# leaves this machine.
#
# pre-commit only formats *staged* files (lint-staged), which leaves three
# holes that all showed up in practice: a merge commit (lint-staged's
# stash-based flow is unsafe mid-merge, so merge commits routinely skip it),
# any `--no-verify`, and the fact that nothing ever looked at the tree AS A
# WHOLE. CI was therefore the first thing to check the repository, which is
# exactly why the recurring symptom was "prettier passes locally, CI fails" --
# not a difference between the two prettiers (same 3.9.6, same config, and no
# CRLF involved), but a file that no local command had ever been asked about.
#
# These are the same invocations as .github/workflows/ci.yml's `format` and
# `lint` jobs, so a green pre-push means those two jobs are green. Build,
# tests, migrations and the coverage gate stay in CI on purpose: they need
# minutes and a Postgres service, and a push-time hook that slow gets
# bypassed, which would put us right back here.
# `set -e` is load-bearing: without it the hook returns only the LAST
# command's status, so a failing format:check followed by a passing lint
# exits 0 and the push sails through -- a guard that does not block.
# Verified by breaking the formatting on purpose and watching this fail.
set -e

npm run format:check
npm run lint
